'''Tilieæ''' (ti-lī′ē-ē), ''n. pl.'' [NL. (Bentham and Hooker, 1862), < ''Tilia'' + ''-eæ.''] A tribe of plants, of the order ''Tiliaceæ.'' {{smaller|It is characterized by flowers with distinct sepals, and colored petals inserted closely around the stamens. It includes 14 genera, among which the chief are ''Tilia'' (the type), ''Sparmannia, Corchorus,'' and ''Muntingia.''}}

'''tilier†''', ''n.'' A Middle English form of ''tiller''<sup>1</sup>.

'''tiling''' (tī′ling), ''n.'' [Verbal n. of ''tile''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' The operation of covering or roofing with tiles. —'''2.''' An assemblage of tiles, as on a roof; tiles collectively or in general.

{{smaller block|They went upon the housetop, and let him down through the ''tiling'' with his couch into the midst before Jesus.
{{right|Luke v. 19.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Asphalt tiling.''' See ''asphalt.''}}

