'''till'''<sup>3</sup>† (til), ''v.'' [< ME. ''tillen'', ''tyllen'', ''tullen'' (also ''tollen'', > E. ''toll''<sup>2</sup>), pull, allure, < AS. *''tillan'', in comp. *''fortillan'', spelled ''for-tyllan'', lead astray, deceive (occurring only once), = OFries. ''tilla'' = MD. D. ''tillen'' = LG. ''tillen'', lift, move from its place, = Sw. dial. ''tille'', take up (''tille på sig'', take upon oneself, lay hold of); other connections uncertain. Hence ''tiller''<sup>2</sup>. Cf. ''toll''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To draw; pull; hence, to entice; allure.

{{smaller block|Then went Mary & Ioseph al-so,
With cherising thai spac him to,
