'''till'''<sup>3</sup> (til), ''n.'' [Early mod. E. ''tyll''; < ''till''<sup>3</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' A drawer; a tray, as of a trunk or box. Also called ''tiller''.

{{smaller block|Closets; and in them many a chest; . . .
In those chests, boxes; in each box, a ''till''.
{{right|''G. Herbert'', The Temple, Confession.}}}}

readings.c3s2:
Specifically—2. A money-drawer; a drawer under or in a shop-counter, in which money is kept.

{{smaller block|They break up counters, doors, and ''tills.'' {{right|''Swift.''}}

It [the dust] treasured itself up, too, in the half-open ''till'', where there still lingered a base sixpence.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, ii.}}}}

3. In ''printing'': (''a'') In earlier forms of hand printing-presses, a crosspiece extending between the main uprights of the frame, and serving to guide and steady the hose or sleeve, which contained the spindle and screws. Also called ''shelf.'' (''b'') One of the spaces or cells between the ribbed projections of the platen of a hand-press.

'''till'''<sup>4</sup> (til), ''n.'' [Origin obscure.] In ''geol.'', a stiff clay containing boulders of all sizes up to several tons in weight, and these often smoothed and striated by glacial action. {{smaller|The word first became current among geologists, with this meaning, in Scotland, but it is now occasionally used elsewhere. Also called ''boulder-clay''.}}

'''tillable''' (til′a-bl), ''a.'' [< ''till''<sup>1</sup> + ''-able.''] Capable of being tilled; arable; fit for the plow.

{{smaller block|The ''tillable'' fields are in some places so hilly that the oxen can hardly take sure footing.
{{right|''R. Carew'', Survey of Cornwall, fol. 20.}}}}

